---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/materials/"
  title="Packaging Materials | Board Grades | Lip Boxes"
  description="Board grades and weights for lip packaging: SBS, kraft, recycled, black-through, metallised, corrugated and greyboard."
  h1="Packaging Materials"
  eyebrow="Specification"
  lead="The board you choose decides how your print reproduces, how the pack holds its shape and how the finished carton is disposed of. This is what each grade actually does."
  ogTitle="Board Grades for Lip Product Packaging"
  ogDescription="How SBS, kraft, recycled, black-through, metallised and corrugated board differ in print, strength and disposal."
  points={['Six board families', 'Weights from 14pt to 3 mm greyboard', 'Trade-offs stated plainly']}
  ctaHeading="Not sure which board suits your product?"
  ctaBody="Describe how the pack is sold and handled, and we will recommend a grade and weight with the reasoning, plus a proof on the actual stock before you commit."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="how-to-choose"
    heading="How to choose a board"
    intro="Three questions settle it in almost every case, and they are worth answering in this order."
  >
    <ol class="ordered">
      <li>
        <strong>Does the print need to be bright?</strong> If shoppers have to
        distinguish six shades at arm's length, you need a coated white surface.
        If the range is monochrome or naturally toned, uncoated works.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What weight is the pack carrying?</strong> One lip balm stick is
        almost nothing. A four-tube multipack, a glass gloss tube or a weighted
        lipstick case all need more board than the size of the carton suggests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Does disposal matter to your positioning?</strong> If it does,
        that constrains the finish more than the board, and it is better decided
        now than after artwork.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="board-grades"
    heading="The board grades we use"
    intro="Six families cover every product on this site. The differences that matter in practice are colour accuracy, cut-edge appearance and what a laminate does to recyclability."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Board grades used for lip packaging, with their practical characteristics."
      columns={['Grade', 'Surface', 'Colour reproduction', 'Typical use']}
      rows={[
        [
          'SBS folding boxboard',
          'Bleached, coated one or two sides',
          'Closest to proof; holds fine halftones and small type',
          'The default for full-colour cosmetic cartons',
        ],
        [
          'Kraft board',
          'Unbleached, uncoated, visible fibre',
          'Warmer and darker throughout; no true white without an opaque underprint',
          'Natural, unbleached and low-waste ranges',
        ],
        [
          'Recycled greyback',
          'White coated face, grey reverse',
          'Good on the print face, marginally duller than SBS',
          'Cost-sensitive volume work and recycled-content claims',
        ],
        [
          'Black-through board',
          'Coloured in the pulp, black throughout',
          'Darker to print onto; needs opaque white for light colours',
          'Dark cartons where white cut edges would show',
        ],
        [
          'Metallised board',
          'Reflective metallised layer under the print',
          'Translucent inks pick up the reflection; opaque white required for normal colours',
          'Holographic and mirror-finish limited editions',
        ],
        [
          'E-flute corrugated',
          'Printed liner over a fine flute',
          'Slightly softer than flat board',
          'Counter displays, shippers and direct mailers',
        ],
        [
          'Greyboard with printed wrap',
          'Rigid core, decorative paper wrap',
          'As good as the wrap stock allows',
          'Rigid gift boxes and presentation cases',
        ],
      ]}
    />
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="weights"
    heading="Board weights and what they hold"
    intro="Weight matters more than grade for strength. These are the starting points we work from, adjusted against your actual packed weight."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Board weight guidance by pack type."
      columns={['Weight', 'Suits', 'Notes']}
      rows={[
        ['14pt', 'Very light single-item cartons inside a shipper', 'Feels thin if handled at a shelf edge'],
        ['16–18pt', 'A single lip balm stick, gloss tube or lipstick bullet', 'The most common specification on this site'],
        ['20–22pt', 'Cartons over three inches tall, or holding two items', 'Also the baseline for a window carton'],
        ['24pt', 'Multipacks, three-packs, glass primaries, hanging packs', 'Noticeably more substantial in the hand'],
        ['28pt and above', 'Heavier sets and premium single cartons', 'Diminishing returns on strength; mostly a feel decision'],
        ['E-flute', 'Counter displays, shippers, direct mailers', 'Different construction rules from flat board'],
        ['1.5–3 mm greyboard', 'Rigid boxes', 'Thickness chosen against box size and contents weight'],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="note measure">
      Send us the weight of the <em>packed</em> carton rather than the empty one.
      That single figure decides board weight, base style and, on a hanging pack,
      whether the tab needs reinforcement.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="cut-edges"
    heading="Cut edges, and why black-through board exists"
    intro="This is the detail that most often surprises brands after their first dark carton arrives."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A standard board is white in the middle. Print it black on the outside and
      every die-cut edge and every crease shows a thin white line. On a pale
      carton nobody notices. On a black soft-touch pack it is the first thing
      anyone sees, and it is impossible to fix after production.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      Black-through board is coloured throughout the pulp, so the edges stay
      dark. It costs more per sheet and it is darker to print onto, which means
      light colours need an opaque white layer underneath. On a dark
      <a href="/hang-tab-lipstick-boxes/">window carton for peg display</a> the
      window rim sits at eye level, which is exactly where that white line is
      most visible, so the premium is usually worth paying there.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="liners"
    heading="Liners for paper tubes"
    intro="Rolled paper tubes have a material decision that flat cartons do not: the barrier layer between your product and the board."
  >
    <ul class="bullets measure">
      <li>
        A standard grease-resistant liner handles firm beeswax and butter
        formulas without oil migrating into the paper.
      </li>
      <li>
        Balms high in liquid oils, or poured warm and left soft, need a heavier
        barrier grade.
      </li>
      <li>
        The liner is a coating rather than a separate plastic sleeve, and whether
        a specific grade is compatible with kerbside paper recycling varies by
        material and by municipality.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure">
      We confirm exactly which liner is quoted on your job so any plastic-free
      wording you use is accurate. If you are choosing between formats, the
      <a href="/paper-lip-balm-tubes/">rolled paperboard tube page</a> sets out
      how the construction behaves in use.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="disposal"
    heading="Recyclability, stated accurately"
    intro="All the paperboard grades here are compatible with standard US kerbside paper recycling in their plain or varnished state. What complicates that is what goes on top."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="What each addition does to the disposal route."
      columns={['Addition', 'Effect on paper recycling', 'Alternative']}
      rows={[
        ['Varnish only', 'No practical effect', '—'],
        ['Plastic lamination (matte, gloss, soft-touch)', 'Consumer must separate the film; many streams reject it', 'Matte or satin varnish'],
        ['Clear window patch', 'A second material to separate', 'Printed swatch instead of a window'],
        ['Metallised or holographic layer', 'Not paper-recyclable', 'Holo foil on a small area, or pearlescent ink'],
        ['Foil stamping', 'Small foil areas are generally tolerated', 'Emboss with no ink or foil'],
        ['Magnets in a rigid box', 'Must be removed first, which few consumers do', 'Tuck flap or ribbon closure'],
        ['Foam or flocked insert', 'Separate material', 'Die-cut card platform or moulded pulp'],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="note measure">
      Recycled content and recyclability are different things and get conflated
      often. We will tell you the recycled percentage a specific stock carries
      rather than making a general claim, and we would rather you understated a
      claim than made one you cannot substantiate.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="next"
    heading="Once the board is settled"
    intro="Material constrains what the rest of the specification can achieve, which is why it comes first."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      With a grade and weight chosen, the next decisions are the press and the
      coating. Which press suits you depends almost entirely on your quantity and
      colour count, set out on the
      <a href="/printing-options/">printing options page</a>, and the coatings
      that each board supports are covered under finishes.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
